  "Question": "The values of \\(y\\) which will satisfy the equations\n\n\\[ \\begin{aligned} 2x^2+6x+5y+1&=0,\\\\ 2x+y+3&=0 \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nmay be found by solving:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(y^2+14y-7=0\\)",
    "B": "\\(y^2+8y+1=0\\)",
    "C": "\\(y^2+10y-7=0\\)",
    "D": "\\(y^2+y-12=0\\)",
    "E": "None of the above equations"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because the choices are equations in \\(y\\) alone, use the linear equation to eliminate \\(x\\). From \\(2x+y+3=0\\),\n\\[\nx=-\\frac{y+3}{2}.\n\\]\nSubstitute this into \\(2x^2+6x+5y+1=0\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{(y+3)^2}{2}-3(y+3)+5y+1=0.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) removes the denominator. Expanding and combining like terms gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y+3)^2-6(y+3)+10y+2&=0,\\\\\ny^2+6y+9-6y-18+10y+2&=0,\\\\\ny^2+10y-7&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll steps are reversible: any root \\(y\\) of this equation, paired with \\(x=-(y+3)/2\\), satisfies both original equations. Thus the required equation is \\(\\boxed{y^2+10y-7=0}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
